lesson "Operations on Functions" {
  course: "algebra"
  skills: ["func-operations", "func-composition"]

  slide "Operations on Functions" {
    id: "l3-intro"
    cat: "Basics"
    > Just like numbers, we can add, subtract, multiply, and divide functions.
    > We can also compose them!
  }

  slide "Adding Functions" {
    id: "l3-add"
    cat: "Addition"
    > If f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = 3x, then (f + g)(x) = (x + 2) + 3x = 4x + 2.
    > We simply add their outputs.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 5]
      y: [-10, 20]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 1 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x + 2 { color: neutral, width: 2, style: dashed }
      curve g = 3*x { color: neutral, width: 2, style: dashed }
      curve sum = 4*x + 2 { color: primary, width: 4 }
      point p = (t, 4*t + 2) { color: danger, r: 6, label: "f+g" }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Subtracting Functions" {
    id: "l3-sub"
    cat: "Subtraction"
    > f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x. (f - g)(x) = (2x + 1) - x = x + 1.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 5]
      y: [-5, 10]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 1 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = 2*x + 1 { color: neutral, width: 2, style: dashed }
      curve g = x { color: neutral, width: 2, style: dashed }
      curve diff = x + 1 { color: danger, width: 4 }
      point p = (t, t + 1) { color: primary, r: 6, label: "f-g" }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Multiplying Functions" {
    id: "l3-mul"
    cat: "Multiplication"
    > f(x) = x and g(x) = x + 1. (f * g)(x) = x(x + 1) = x² + x.
    > Multiplication can change the type of function (linear to quadratic).

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 5]
      y: [-5, 20]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 1 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve f = x { color: neutral, width: 2, style: dashed }
      curve g = x + 1 { color: neutral, width: 2, style: dashed }
      curve prod = x^2 + x { color: accent, width: 4 }
      point p = (t, t^2 + t) { color: danger, r: 6, label: "f*g" }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Dividing Functions" {
    id: "l3-div"
    cat: "Division"
    > Be careful with division! (f / g)(x) = f(x) / g(x). The domain excludes
    > values where g(x) = 0 — here the hole at x = 1 marks that gap.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 5]
      y: [-5, 10]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 2 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve quot = x + 1 { color: success, width: 4 }
      circle hole = (1, 2) { r: 0.12, color: success }
      point p = (t, t + 1) { color: danger, r: 6 }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }

  slide "Quiz: Division Domain" {
    id: "l3-quiz"
    cat: "Assessment"
    quiz {
      ask "For (f / g)(x) = (x² - 1) / (x - 1), what value is excluded from the domain?"
      - "x = 0"
      * "x = 1"
      - "x = -1"
      - "x = 2"
    }
  }

  slide "Composition of Functions" {
    id: "l3-comp"
    cat: "Composition"
    > Composition (f ∘ g)(x) means applying g first, then f. f(g(x)).
    > If f(x) = x + 2 and g(x) = 3x, then f(g(x)) = 3x + 2.

    scene plane {
      x: [-5, 5]
      y: [-10, 20]
      grid
      axes
      param t = 1 { range: [-3, 3], step: 0.1 }
      curve comp = 3*x + 2 { color: warning, width: 4 }
      point p = (t, 3*t + 2) { color: primary, r: 6, label: "f(g(x))" }
      slider t { label: "Input x" }
    }
  }
}
